 \paragraph{Proposal covariance.} The archive $A_n$ is the top-$k$ history members by smallest discrepancy. Component weights are formed in log-space as $\tilde W_j \propto w_j\,K_{\epsilon_n}(\rho_j)$ and normalized (a uniform fallback is used if all smooth weights underflow). The perturbation covariance is $\Sigma_n = s\,[\,\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}_{\tilde W}(A_n) + \lambda_n I\,]$ with $s$ the \texttt{perturbation\_scale} and a two-part jitter $\lambda_n = 10^{-9}\,\mathrm{tr}\,\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}_{\tilde W}(A_n)/d + 10^{-12}\,\bar b^2$, where $\bar b^2$ is the mean squared prior-box width; it is Cholesky-factored once per call. The first term is scale-aware, so a tightly converged archive (marginal standard deviation ${\sim}10^{-4}$) is not swamped by the floor as a fixed $10^{-6}$ would swamp it; the second is a run-independent absolute floor that keeps the matrix factorable for a degenerate archive and supplies the uniform lower spectral bound Assumption~\ref{ass:class} needs (Table~\ref{tab:assumption-status}). Each mixture component is truncated to the prior box, with its log normalizing mass computed as the product of the per-axis in-box masses. That is exact for a diagonal covariance, in which case the proposal integrates to one on the support; for a correlated covariance it is an approximation, so the evaluated proposal is a positive function that need not integrate to one. This is why \S\ref{sec:theory} states its denominator assumption for positive functions and assigns the discrepancy to factor~(c) of $r$ (\S\ref{sec:theory-r}).
 
